Negative control for the picker being a CHECKBOX list (`R7-3`).

Takes the package's checkable menu item off the picker and hand-rolls the entry
it used to compose: a plain menu item with a tick drawn beside the label, so an
entry that is on carries a glyph, an entry that is off carries nothing at all,
and there is no box to aim at. The menu reads as a list of words rather than a
list of controls, which is what the operator was shown.

Every BEHAVIOUR survives — the same entries in the same order, the same emitted
set, the same last-column floor, the same refusal to open while a track is armed
— which is the point: the defect is what the entry IS, not what it does. The one
law withdrawn is the item's own checkable semantics, so `aria-checked` is the
only thing that stops answering.

The mutation is on the CONSUMER because that is the only side this lane owns:
`packages/ui` is import-only (`R7-15`), and the library's checkable item is
already approved. A picker that composes it correctly is the whole of what is
provable here.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the column-picker spec's two ticked-set cases — the entries no longer say
which columns the table is drawing, while every behaviour case stays green.

